macOS: %gs:0x08 and %gs:0x10 (NT_TIB.StackBase, StackLimit) are PE-owned across the crossings, and the
PE ntdll reads the stack bounds there.

Windows keeps NT_TIB.StackBase and StackLimit at %gs:0x08 and %gs:0x10, and a program that runs its own
fiber system writes them on every switch (Boost.Context style). On macOS those slots belong to the host
thread block: the errno pointer and the MIG reply port. Microsoft Flight Simulator 2024 wrote its fiber's
stack base there, and the first Wine unix routine that touched errno on that thread read through it
(ntdll.so, movl (%rax) after __error(), rax = 0x15010000, M4, macOS 27.0, 2026-10-09,
gin/private/notes/msfs-2024-2026-10-08.md section H); the sim's own handlers then reported "FiberError".

Now the two slots take turns like %gs:0x20 (0009/0022) and %gs:0x68 (0011): on every entry to unix code
PE's values go to teb->Instrumentation[1] and [2] and the host's errno pointer and reply port (kept in
[3] and [4]) come back; on every return to PE code the reverse. They start as the TEB's StackBase and
StackLimit (call_init_thunk), the unix guard-page move keeps [2] in step, and kernelbase's own
SwitchToFiber writes the slots as it writes the fields (the same addresses where %gs is the TEB). The PE
ntdll's exception dispatcher (is_valid_frame, the unwind end checks) reads the bounds from the slots,
which is where Windows reads them, so a fiber stack a program announced there is accepted without the
tsshim wide-bounds experiment.

D3DMetal's host code called directly from PE code sees PE's values in those slots, as it did before this
patch whenever such a program ran. Unverified when written. Trial, branch msfs-gsbase.
